<h2>The question</h2>
<p>Every review finding is judged by the same bar. Should strictness depend on the kind of defect and on how far
the work has been released, and if so how is the deferred part tracked so it is not forgotten?</p>

<h2>What our own record shows</h2>
<p>Window: 2026-10-03 to 2026-10-17, the web-everything repository only. 5,971 unique findings on 869 PRs,
labelled into 42 classes by model judgement (about 10 to 15 percent boundary mislabels expected).</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Number</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Missing test for a stated guarantee</td><td>794 findings (13.3%); 52.9 of 341 attributed fix rounds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guard weakened or bypassable</td><td>336</td></tr>
<tr><td>Doc, skill or card drift</td><td>291; 76% false rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ruled findings that were not real</td><td>257 of 780 (33%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Auto-block rulings over a reviewer not-real</td><td>126 of 134</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fix rounds with a single cause</td><td>51 of 341; most bundle 3 to 5 reasons</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>No secret, injection, authorization, data-loss or local-path finding stalled delivery in the window. Timeouts,
races, unbounded reads, non-hermetic tests and recovery paths did.</p>

<h2>Outside models</h2>
<ul>
<li>OWASP ASVS: requirements carry the lowest level they apply to; stricter levels inherit.</li>
<li>CVSS v4.0: severity is an intrinsic part plus an environmental modifier.</li>
<li>Google SRE error budget policy: when the budget is spent, changes halt except P0 and security fixes (verified).</li>
<li>SQALE and the Fowler quadrant: debt carries a fix cost and a cost of not fixing; only prudent and deliberate debt is acceptable (quadrant verified).</li>
<li>Sonar: gate on new code, keep old debt separate (verified on the current docs page).</li>
<li>AI-code failure modes: hallucinated packages, insecure completions (Pearce, Perry verified), test gaming, over-broad edits. The AIDev dataset abstract gives lower acceptance of agent PRs but no rejection reasons.</li>
<li>DORA now lists five metrics, including deployment rework rate (corrected from four).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Proposal</h2>
<p>Four outcomes (block, expedite, debt, advisory), four levels (internal delivery, dogfood, public preview, public
release) where each level inherits the blocks below it, a class-by-level table in settings, and a debt register
whose entries gate the move up a level. See the decision item for the table and the forks.</p>
<p>Decision card: <a href="/backlog/xugqjw1-decision-review-stringency-classified-findings-per-level-out/">review stringency decision</a>.</p>
